narratives (i.e., temporal scenes). We illustrate this behaviour with Bayesian belief updating – and neuronal process theories – to simulate the epistemic foraging seen in reading. These simulations reproduce perisaccadic delay period activity and local field potentials seen empirically. Finally, we exploit the deep structure of these models to simulate responses to local (e.g., font type) and global (e.g., semantic) violations; reproducing mismatch negativity and P300 responses respectively."}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "a5269dee11e4de408d9a60de6ae72a46d85f940ac36b50d74a98b00a97ab3cfc", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "emergent_preferences", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.1016/j.neubiorev.2016.06.022", "doi:10.1016/j.neubiorev.2018.04.004", "openalex:W2463330366"], "source_candidates": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neubiorev.2016.06.022", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763416301336/pdf", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763418302525/pdf", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neubiorev.2018.04.004"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}
